### What is the Decorator pattern?

The Decorator pattern attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically by wrapping it in a decorator that implements the same interface. The decorator delegates the core call to the wrapped object and adds its own contribution before or after.

```java
package com.bank.decorator;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface AccountFeature {
    BigDecimal monthlyFee();
    String describe();
}
```

### Why does the Decorator pattern exist?

Without decorators, combining optional features requires a subclass for every combination. With three features (overdraft, rewards, insurance) you would need:

- `BasicAccount`
- `OverdraftAccount`
- `RewardsAccount`
- `InsuranceAccount`
- `OverdraftRewardsAccount`
- `OverdraftInsuranceAccount`
- `RewardsInsuranceAccount`
- `OverdraftRewardsInsuranceAccount`

That is 2^3 = 8 subclasses, and every new feature doubles the count. Decorators collapse this to one class per feature.

```java
// Without decorators: 8 classes for 3 features (bad)
class OverdraftRewardsInsuranceAccount extends BasicAccount { }

// With decorators: 3 classes, composed freely (good)
new RewardsFeature(new OverdraftFeature(new BasicAccount()))
```

### How do you implement the base component?

The base component implements the interface and provides the default behaviour.

```java
package com.bank.decorator;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BasicAccount implements AccountFeature {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyFee() {
        return new BigDecimal("100.00"); // base maintenance fee
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return "Basic Account";
    }
}
```

### How do you implement decorators?

Each decorator implements the same interface, holds a reference to the wrapped component, and adds its own contribution.

```java
package com.bank.decorator;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OverdraftFeature implements AccountFeature {
    private final AccountFeature wrapped;

    public OverdraftFeature(AccountFeature wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyFee() {
        return wrapped.monthlyFee().add(new BigDecimal("50.00"));
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return wrapped.describe() + " + Overdraft Protection";
    }
}

public class RewardsFeature implements AccountFeature {
    private final AccountFeature wrapped;

    public RewardsFeature(AccountFeature wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyFee() {
        return wrapped.monthlyFee().add(new BigDecimal("75.00"));
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return wrapped.describe() + " + Premium Rewards";
    }
}

public class InsuranceFeature implements AccountFeature {
    private final AccountFeature wrapped;

    public InsuranceFeature(AccountFeature wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyFee() {
        return wrapped.monthlyFee().add(new BigDecimal("200.00"));
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return wrapped.describe() + " + Life Insurance";
    }
}
```

### How do you compose decorators at runtime?

Stack decorators in any combination to match what the customer wants.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.decorator.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DecoratorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Rajesh: basic + overdraft + rewards
        AccountFeature rajesh = new RewardsFeature(
            new OverdraftFeature(new BasicAccount()));
        System.out.println(rajesh.describe());
        System.out.println("Monthly fee: INR " + rajesh.monthlyFee());

        // Priya: basic + insurance
        AccountFeature priya = new InsuranceFeature(new BasicAccount());
        System.out.println(priya.describe());
        System.out.println("Monthly fee: INR " + priya.monthlyFee());
    }
}
```

```text
Basic Account + Overdraft Protection + Premium Rewards
Monthly fee: INR 225.00
Basic Account + Life Insurance
Monthly fee: INR 300.00
```

### How is Decorator different from inheritance?

| Aspect | Decorator | Inheritance |
|---|---|---|
| Binding | Runtime composition | Compile-time hierarchy |
| Combinations | Stack any subset freely | One subclass per combination |
| Growth | n features = n classes | n features = 2^n classes |
| Adding a feature | New decorator class, zero changes | Edit or extend many subclasses |
| Flexibility | Order and selection at runtime | Fixed at compile time |

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is making the decorator depend on the concrete component instead of the interface. This prevents stacking.

```java
// Wrong: depends on BasicAccount, cannot wrap other decorators
public class OverdraftFeature {
    private final BasicAccount account; // too specific
}

// Correct: depends on the interface, can wrap anything
public class OverdraftFeature implements AccountFeature {
    private final AccountFeature wrapped; // abstract
}
```

The second mistake is overly deep stacks that hurt readability. Keep the decorator count reasonable and consider a builder when the configuration becomes complex.

### When should you use the Decorator pattern?

Use it when a base object has many optional features that customers want to combine freely, when subclassing would explode, or when you need to add behaviour without modifying existing classes.

Do not use it when there is only one fixed configuration, or when the overhead of wrapper objects matters in a performance-critical path.

```java
// Good use case: optional account add-ons
AccountFeature acc = new RewardsFeature(
    new OverdraftFeature(new BasicAccount()));

// Bad use case: only one configuration exists
AccountFeature acc = new BasicAccount(); // no decorators needed
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Ordering matters when one decorator's output feeds another. In the account example, fees are additive so order does not matter. But in a notification system where encryption runs before logging, the order determines whether the log shows encrypted or plain text. Keep decorators commutative where possible and document the order where it is significant.

At National Bank of India, Pankaj models optional account add-ons like overdraft, rewards, and insurance as decorators, so Priya can mix and match features and the monthly fee composes automatically, without the bank ever maintaining a class for every combination.

```java
// Ordering example: encryption before vs after logging
Notifier v1 = new LoggingDecorator(new EncryptionDecorator(sms));
// Logs: plain text, sends: encrypted

Notifier v2 = new EncryptionDecorator(new LoggingDecorator(sms));
// Logs: encrypted text, sends: encrypted
```
